module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.the_grant_a_frontier_refuses_is_already_held_from_another_authority

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data the_grant_a_frontier_refuses_is_already_held_from_another_authority: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "the_grant_a_frontier_refuses_is_already_held_from_another_authority" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "INVALID STATE: one authority DECLINES to grant a capability to a principal, states the harm the grant would cause, and blocks its own consumer on a frontier until a safer principal exists -- while a SECOND authority over the same principal on the same host already grants that capability, wider. The refusal is then a wall with no back, and the belief it creates in every reader of the declining module -- \"this principal cannot do that\" -- is FALSE and recorded nowhere. Nothing refuses, because the two authorities never meet: each is internally coherent, the narrowing one is the one people read, and the widening one is older and lives in a module about a different subject.",

    "HARM, AND WHY IT IS WORSE THAN AN UNGUARDED SYSTEM. An honest absence of a wall gets budgeted; a wall that is believed makes everything downstream of it cheap to justify. Work is sequenced against the frontier, reviews cite it as the reason a change is safe, and a rung is reported at the strength of the narrowing authority while the actual rung is the WIDER grant's. That is DESIGN section 4b(1) rung inflation applied to an authorization rather than to a compiler class -- and an inflated class never ranks for climbing, so the real grant is never closed.",

    "SPECIMEN, verified 2026-09-21 on live srv1. gunbc.executor_privileged_operation exists to make an authorization and the argv it authorizes one fact, and it states the rule in its own header: `a binary path alone cannot say which directory it may create or who ends up owning it, and granting whole /usr/bin/install is granting root`. Every line of /etc/sudoers.d/gunbc-ghrunner is therefore derived from a parameterized operation, argv spelled in full. gunbc.runner_microvm_network_converge microvm_network_granted_operations then goes further and withholds the MUTATING half of the microVM network converge from the job user entirely, granting the readbacks only, because `install <grantee-writable path> /etc/systemd/system/...` beside `systemctl restart` on that unit is arbitrary root for anything running in a job (review 69104); microvm_network_host_mutation_principal_frontier blocks the apply half on an administrator principal the job user cannot impersonate. MEANWHILE, on the same host, /etc/sudoers.d/gunbc-deploy -- GENERATED by gunbc.ci_deploy_sudoers from the gunbc.fleet_posix_accounts fleet_posix_ci_runner_sudo_grants roster, whose members are BINARY PATHS with no argv -- carries `ghrunner ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD:` over /usr/bin/install, /usr/bin/systemctl, /usr/bin/rm, /usr/sbin/visudo, /usr/sbin/usermod, /usr/sbin/useradd, /usr/bin/apt-get and /usr/bin/tailscale, path-unrestricted. The exact install+systemctl pair the frontier refuses to grant is already held, unbounded, by the same job user; visudo and usermod make it durable. ghrunner is root-equivalent on srv1 today, and ghrunner is the principal every CI step runs as, pull requests included.",

    "THE DISTINGUISHING FACTS, so this is not read as ordinary over-permission. (1) TWO AUTHORITIES, ONE SUBJECT: the subject is one POSIX principal on one host, and two modules each render a sudoers file for it. (2) OPPOSITE GRAINS: one is argv-pinned by construction, the other is path-only, so the wider file is not a superset of the narrower one at the same grain -- it is a different KIND of grant, which is why no roster comparison between them would have reported anything. (3) THE NARROWING AUTHORITY NAMES THE HARM AND IS RIGHT ABOUT IT: it is not a stale opinion, it is the current design argument, and it is reasoning about a capability the principal already has. (4) THE BELIEF IS LOAD-BEARING AND UNRECORDED: no gunbc.rung_drop, guarantee_stall or recurring_failure_mode row cited gunbc.ci_deploy_sudoers for it before this one, so the gap existed only in the world and in nobody's ledger. (5) IT IS NOT DISCOVERABLE FROM THE DECLINING MODULE: nothing in gunbc.runner_microvm_network_converge's import closure reaches gunbc.ci_deploy_sudoers, and nothing ever will -- they are legitimately different layers.",

    "RECOGNITION RULE. When a module refuses to grant a capability to a named principal, ask what that principal ALREADY holds on the subject host, read from the host or from every authority that renders an authorization for it -- not from the refusing module's own closure, which by construction cannot see the answer. The tell is a refusal whose justification is a CAPABILITY sentence (`this would be arbitrary root`) rather than a SET sentence (`this principal holds exactly X`): a capability sentence is a claim about the whole principal and is falsified by any other grant anywhere, while the author only checked the grants their module renders.",

    "RUNG FOUND AT: mitigatable, and generously so -- the containment was entirely in the wider grant's consumers being trusted today, which is a property of who currently runs jobs rather than of any modeled structure. ATTAINABLE CEILING: structural impossibility, and it is decidable: whether a principal holds a path-unrestricted sudo grant is a property of a rendered sudoers body, and both bodies are RENDERED FROM MODELED ROSTERS IN THIS REPOSITORY. A PosixSudoGrant whose authorization is a bare command_path has no way to say which argv it admits, so the invalid state is writable only because that type admits a path without one. Making the grant carry its argv -- as gunbc.executor_privileged_operation already does for the other file -- removes the constructor rather than adding a check.",

    "NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, at capability grain and deliberately not naming an artifact. EVERY sudoers body this repository renders for a runner-host principal is derived from an operation that pins its complete argv, so a path-only grant for that principal has no spelling -- which makes the union over ALL authorities rendering for one principal a set the corpus can state, and makes a capability refusal like microvm_network_host_mutation_principal_frontier checkable against it rather than against one module's roster. WHAT DOES NOT SATISFY IT: a lens comparing the two rendered files (validation where construction is available, and it reports only the hosts whose files someone thought to compare); narrowing /etc/sudoers.d/gunbc-deploy by hand on one host (the population is every host the deploy preamble has run on, and the generator would rewrite it); or deleting the frontier because the grant it refuses is already held -- that is the inversion, and it converts a true statement about a wall into permission to stop building it.",

    "THE STANDING LOSS IS ROSTERED SEPARATELY, because a class is not a declared drop: gunbc.rung_drop the_job_user_holds_a_path_unrestricted_root_grant carries the bounded population and the restoration trigger. CLOSING THE GRANTS IS A SEPARATE ITEM and is not this row's repair: legacy jobs on the host consume them today, so narrowing them needs a consumer census of each grant and an operator decision, and it is what gates untrusted-PR qualification of the microVM host.",

    "CONSUMPTION: this per-class record joins the derived gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.roster recurring_failure_mode_roster.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
